{
  "image_name": "1c975005792ae7d00a70d55b2ce70055.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes how cancer driver genes are identified from tumour sequencing data across many patients. It begins with cell-to-cell variation, where a mutation in a driver gene gives one clone a growth advantage, leading to selection and expansion of mutant cells in a tumour. Somatic mutations are then found by comparing a tumour sample with a matched normal sample from the same patient, yielding many mutations per genome or exome.\n\nAcross a patient cohort, these mutations are organized into a gene-by-patient matrix showing that each tumour carries mutations in a subset of genes. The main goal is to distinguish positively selected driver genes from the large background of passenger mutations. The figure illustrates several visible signals of positive selection: an excess of mutations compared with expectation, clustering along the linear gene sequence, clustering in protein 3D structure, enrichment within a particular functional domain, bias toward mutations with stronger predicted functional impact, and specific trinucleotide-context patterns.\n\nThese signals are combined to produce a cohort-specific catalogue of driver genes and the evidence supporting each one. Example genes such as APC, TP53, KRAS, PIK3CA, and SMAD2 show different combinations of selection signals, emphasizing that driver detection relies on multiple complementary patterns rather than mutation counts alone.",
